[31m-[m
[31m-**Example: Transforming numeric values**[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```js[m
[31m-const numeric = braces.expand('{1..5}', {[m
[31m-  transform(value) {[m
[31m-    // when numeric values are passed, "value" is a number[m
[31m-    return 'foo/' + value * 2;[m
[31m-  },[m
[31m-});[m
[31m-console.log(numeric);[m
[31m-//=> [ 'foo/2', 'foo/4', 'foo/6', 'foo/8', 'foo/10' ][m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### options.quantifiers[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**Type**: `Boolean`[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**Default**: `undefined`[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**Description**: In regular expressions, quanitifiers can be used to specify how many times a token can be repeated. For example, `a{1,3}` will match the letter `a` one to three times.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Unfortunately, regex quantifiers happen to share the same syntax as [Bash lists](#lists)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-The `quantifiers` option tells braces to detect when [regex quantifiers](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/RegExp#quantifiers) are defined in the given pattern, and not to try to expand them as lists.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**Examples**[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```js[m
[31m-const braces = require('braces');[m
[31m-console.log(braces('a/b{1,3}/{x,y,z}'));[m
[31m-//=> [ 'a/b(1|3)/(x|y|z)' ][m
[31m-console.log(braces('a/b{1,3}/{x,y,z}', { quantifiers: true }));[m
[31m-//=> [ 'a/b{1,3}/(x|y|z)' ][m
[31m-console.log(braces('a/b{1,3}/{x,y,z}', { quantifiers: true, expand: true }));[m
[31m-//=> [ 'a/b{1,3}/x', 'a/b{1,3}/y', 'a/b{1,3}/z' ][m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### options.keepEscaping[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**Type**: `Boolean`[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**Default**: `undefined`[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**Description**: Do not strip backslashes that were used for escaping from the result.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## What is "brace expansion"?[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Brace expansion is a type of parameter expansion that was made popular by unix shells for generating lists of strings, as well as regex-like matching when used alongside wildcards (globs).[m
[31m-[m
[31m-In addition to "expansion", braces are also used for matching. In other words:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-- [brace expansion](#brace-expansion) is for generating new lists[m
[31m-- [brace matching](#brace-matching) is for filtering existing lists[m
[31m-[m
[31m-<details>[m
[31m-<summary><strong>More about brace expansion</strong> (click to expand)</summary>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-There are two main types of brace expansion:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-1. **lists**: which are defined using comma-separated values inside curly braces: `{a,b,c}`[m
[31m-2. **sequences**: which are defined using a starting value and an ending value, separated by two dots: `a{1..3}b`. Optionally, a third argument may be passed to define a "step" or increment to use: `a{1..100..10}b`. These are also sometimes referred to as "ranges".[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Here are some example brace patterns to illustrate how they work:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**Sets**[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-{a,b,c}       => a b c[m
[31m-{a,b,c}{1,2}  => a1 a2 b1 b2 c1 c2[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**Sequences**[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-{1..9}        => 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 9[m
[31m-{4..-4}       => 4 3 2 1 0 -1 -2 -3 -4[m
[31m-{1..20..3}    => 1 4 7 10 13 16 19[m
[31m-{a..j}        => a b c d e f g h i j[m
[31m-{j..a}        => j i h g f e d c b a[m
[31m-{a..z..3}     => a d g j m p s v y[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**Combination**[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Sets and sequences can be mixed together or used along with any other strings.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-{a,b,c}{1..3}   => a1 a2 a3 b1 b2 b3 c1 c2 c3[m
[31m-foo/{a,b,c}/bar => foo/a/bar foo/b/bar foo/c/bar[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-The fact that braces can be "expanded" from relatively simple patterns makes them ideal for quickly generating test fixtures, file paths, and similar use cases.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Brace matching[m
[31m-[m
[31m-In addition to _expansion_, brace patterns are also useful for performing regular-expression-like matching.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-For example, the pattern `foo/{1..3}/bar` would match any of following strings:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-foo/1/bar[m
[31m-foo/2/bar[m
[31m-foo/3/bar[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-But not:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-baz/1/qux[m
[31m-baz/2/qux[m
[31m-baz/3/qux[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Braces can also be combined with [glob patterns](https://github.com/jonschlinkert/micromatch) to perform more advanced wildcard matching. For example, the pattern `*/{1..3}/*` would match any of following strings:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-foo/1/bar[m
[31m-foo/2/bar[m
[31m-foo/3/bar[m
[31m-baz/1/qux[m
[31m-baz/2/qux[m
[31m-baz/3/qux[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Brace matching pitfalls[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Although brace patterns offer a user-friendly way of matching ranges or sets of strings, there are also some major disadvantages and potential risks you should be aware of.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### tldr[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**"brace bombs"**[m
[31m-[m
[31m-- brace expansion can eat up a huge amount of processing resources[m
[31m-- as brace patterns increase _linearly in size_, the system resources required to expand the pattern increase exponentially[m
[31m-- users can accidentally (or intentially) exhaust your system's resources resulting in the equivalent of a DoS attack (bonus: no programming knowledge is required!)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-For a more detailed explanation with examples, see the [geometric complexity](#geometric-complexity) section.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### The solution[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Jump to the [performance section](#performance) to see how Braces solves this problem in comparison to other libraries.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### Geometric complexity[m
[31m-[m
[31m-At minimum, brace patterns with sets limited to two elements have quadradic or `O(n^2)` complexity. But the complexity of the algorithm increases exponentially as the number of sets, _and elements per set_, increases, which is `O(n^c)`.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-For example, the following sets demonstrate quadratic (`O(n^2)`) complexity:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-{1,2}{3,4}      => (2X2)    => 13 14 23 24[m
[31m-{1,2}{3,4}{5,6} => (2X2X2)  => 135 136 145 146 235 236 245 246[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-But add an element to a set, and we get a n-fold Cartesian product with `O(n^c)` complexity:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-{1,2,3}{4,5,6}{7,8,9} => (3X3X3) => 147 148 149 157 158 159 167 168 169 247 248[m
[31m-                                    249 257 258 259 267 268 269 347 348 349 357[m
[31m-                                    358 359 367 368 369[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Now, imagine how this complexity grows given that each element is a n-tuple:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-{1..100}{1..100}         => (100X100)     => 10,000 elements (38.4 kB)[m
[31m-{1..100}{1..100}{1..100} => (100X100X100) => 1,000,000 elements (5.76 MB)[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Although these examples are clearly contrived, they demonstrate how brace patterns can quickly grow out of control.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**More information**[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Interested in learning more about brace expansion?[m
[31m-[m
[31m-- [linuxjournal/bash-brace-expansion](http://www.linuxjournal.com/content/bash-brace-expansion)[m
[31m-- [rosettacode/Brace_expansion](https://rosettacode.org/wiki/Brace_expansion)[m
[31m-- [cartesian product](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cartesian_product)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-</details>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Performance[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Braces is not only screaming fast, it's also more accurate the other brace expansion libraries.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### Better algorithms[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Fortunately there is a solution to the ["brace bomb" problem](#brace-matching-pitfalls): _don't expand brace patterns into an array when they're used for matching_.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Instead, convert the pattern into an optimized regular expression. This is easier said than done, and braces is the only library that does this currently.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**The proof is in the numbers**[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Minimatch gets exponentially slower as patterns increase in complexity, braces does not. The following results were generated using `braces()` and `minimatch.braceExpand()`, respectively.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-| **Pattern**                 | **braces**          | **[minimatch][]**            |[m
[31m-| --------------------------- | ------------------- | ---------------------------- |[m
[31m-| `{1..9007199254740991}`[^1] | `298 B` (5ms 459μs) | N/A (freezes)                |[m
[31m-| `{1..1000000000000000}`     | `41 B` (1ms 15μs)   | N/A (freezes)                |[m
[31m-| `{1..100000000000000}`      | `40 B` (890μs)      | N/A (freezes)                |[m
[31m-| `{1..10000000000000}`       | `39 B` (2ms 49μs)   | N/A (freezes)                |[m
[31m-| `{1..1000000000000}`        | `38 B` (608μs)      | N/A (freezes)                |[m
[31m-| `{1..100000000000}`         | `37 B` (397μs)      | N/A (freezes)                |[m
[31m-| `{1..10000000000}`          | `35 B` (983μs)      | N/A (freezes)                |[m
[31m-| `{1..1000000000}`           | `34 B` (798μs)      | N/A (freezes)                |[m
[31m-| `{1..100000000}`            | `33 B` (733μs)      | N/A (freezes)                |[m
[31m-| `{1..10000000}`             | `32 B` (5ms 632μs)  | `78.89 MB` (16s 388ms 569μs) |[m
[31m-| `{1..1000000}`              | `31 B` (1ms 381μs)  | `6.89 MB` (1s 496ms 887μs)   |[m
[31m-| `{1..100000}`               | `30 B` (950μs)      | `588.89 kB` (146ms 921μs)    |[m
[31m-| `{1..10000}`                | `29 B` (1ms 114μs)  | `48.89 kB` (14ms 187μs)      |[m
[31m-| `{1..1000}`                 | `28 B` (760μs)      | `3.89 kB` (1ms 453μs)        |[m
[31m-| `{1..100}`                  | `22 B` (345μs)      | `291 B` (196μs)              |[m
[31m-| `{1..10}`                   | `10 B` (533μs)      | `20 B` (37μs)                |[m
[31m-| `{1..3}`                    | `7 B` (190μs)       | `5 B` (27μs)                 |[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### Faster algorithms[m
[31m-[m
[31m-When you need expansion, braces is still much faster.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-_(the following results were generated using `braces.expand()` and `minimatch.braceExpand()`, respectively)_[m
[31m-[m
