[31m-# Chokidar [![Weekly downloads](https://img.shields.io/npm/dw/chokidar.svg)](https://github.com/paulmillr/chokidar) [![Yearly downloads](https://img.shields.io/npm/dy/chokidar.svg)](https://github.com/paulmillr/chokidar)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-> Minimal and efficient cross-platform file watching library[m
[31m-[m
[31m-[![NPM](https://nodei.co/npm/chokidar.png)](https://www.npmjs.com/package/chokidar)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Why?[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Node.js `fs.watch`:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-* Doesn't report filenames on MacOS.[m
[31m-* Doesn't report events at all when using editors like Sublime on MacOS.[m
[31m-* Often reports events twice.[m
[31m-* Emits most changes as `rename`.[m
[31m-* Does not provide an easy way to recursively watch file trees.[m
[31m-* Does not support recursive watching on Linux.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Node.js `fs.watchFile`:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-* Almost as bad at event handling.[m
[31m-* Also does not provide any recursive watching.[m
[31m-* Results in high CPU utilization.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Chokidar resolves these problems.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Initially made for **[Brunch](https://brunch.io/)** (an ultra-swift web app build tool), it is now used in[m
[31m-[Microsoft's Visual Studio Code](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode),[m
[31m-[gulp](https://github.com/gulpjs/gulp/),[m
[31m-[karma](https://karma-runner.github.io/),[m
[31m-[PM2](https://github.com/Unitech/PM2),[m
[31m-[browserify](http://browserify.org/),[m
[31m-[webpack](https://webpack.github.io/),[m
[31m-[BrowserSync](https://www.browsersync.io/),[m
[31m-and [many others](https://www.npmjs.com/browse/depended/chokidar).[m
[31m-It has proven itself in production environments.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Version 3 is out! Check out our blog post about it: [Chokidar 3: How to save 32TB of traffic every week](https://paulmillr.com/posts/chokidar-3-save-32tb-of-traffic/)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## How?[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Chokidar does still rely on the Node.js core `fs` module, but when using[m
[31m-`fs.watch` and `fs.watchFile` for watching, it normalizes the events it[m
[31m-receives, often checking for truth by getting file stats and/or dir contents.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-On MacOS, chokidar by default uses a native extension exposing the Darwin[m
[31m-`FSEvents` API. This provides very efficient recursive watching compared with[m
[31m-implementations like `kqueue` available on most \*nix platforms. Chokidar still[m
[31m-does have to do some work to normalize the events received that way as well.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-On most other platforms, the `fs.watch`-based implementation is the default, which[m
[31m-avoids polling and keeps CPU usage down. Be advised that chokidar will initiate[m
[31m-watchers recursively for everything within scope of the paths that have been[m
[31m-specified, so be judicious about not wasting system resources by watching much[m
[31m-more than needed.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Getting started[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Install with npm:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```sh[m
[31m-npm install chokidar[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Then `require` and use it in your code:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```javascript[m
[31m-const chokidar = require('chokidar');[m
[31m-[m
[31m-// One-liner for current directory[m
[31m-chokidar.watch('.').on('all', (event, path) => {[m
[31m-  console.log(event, path);[m
[31m-});[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## API[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```javascript[m
[31m-// Example of a more typical implementation structure[m
[31m-[m
[31m-// Initialize watcher.[m
[31m-const watcher = chokidar.watch('file, dir, glob, or array', {[m
[31m-  ignored: /(^|[\/\\])\../, // ignore dotfiles[m
[31m-  persistent: true[m
[31m-});[m
[31m-[m
[31m-// Something to use when events are received.[m
[31m-const log = console.log.bind(console);[m
[31m-// Add event listeners.[m
[31m-watcher[m
[31m-  .on('add', path => log(`File ${path} has been added`))[m
[31m-  .on('change', path => log(`File ${path} has been changed`))[m
[31m-  .on('unlink', path => log(`File ${path} has been removed`));[m
[31m-[m
[31m-// More possible events.[m
[31m-watcher[m
[31m-  .on('addDir', path => log(`Directory ${path} has been added`))[m
[31m-  .on('unlinkDir', path => log(`Directory ${path} has been removed`))[m
[31m-  .on('error', error => log(`Watcher error: ${error}`))[m
[31m-  .on('ready', () => log('Initial scan complete. Ready for changes'))[m
[31m-  .on('raw', (event, path, details) => { // internal[m
[31m-    log('Raw event info:', event, path, details);[m
[31m-  });[m
[31m-[m
[31m-// 'add', 'addDir' and 'change' events also receive stat() results as second[m
[31m-// argument when available: https://nodejs.org/api/fs.html#fs_class_fs_stats[m
[31m-watcher.on('change', (path, stats) => {[m
[31m-  if (stats) console.log(`File ${path} changed size to ${stats.size}`);[m
[31m-});[m
[31m-[m
[31m-// Watch new files.[m
[31m-watcher.add('new-file');[m
[31m-watcher.add(['new-file-2', 'new-file-3', '**/other-file*']);[m
[31m-[m
[31m-// Get list of actual paths being watched on the filesystem[m
[31m-var watchedPaths = watcher.getWatched();[m
[31m-[m
[31m-// Un-watch some files.[m
[31m-await watcher.unwatch('new-file*');[m
[31m-[m
[31m-// Stop watching.[m
[31m-// The method is async![m
[31m-watcher.close().then(() => console.log('closed'));[m
[31m-[m
[31m-// Full list of options. See below for descriptions.[m
[31m-// Do not use this example![m
[31m-chokidar.watch('file', {[m
[31m-  persistent: true,[m
[31m-[m
[31m-  ignored: '*.txt',[m
[31m-  ignoreInitial: false,[m
[31m-  followSymlinks: true,[m
[31m-  cwd: '.',[m
[31m-  disableGlobbing: false,[m
[31m-[m
[31m-  usePolling: false,[m
[31m-  interval: 100,[m
[31m-  binaryInterval: 300,[m
[31m-  alwaysStat: false,[m
[31m-  depth: 99,[m
[31m-  awaitWriteFinish: {[m
[31m-    stabilityThreshold: 2000,[m
[31m-    pollInterval: 100[m
[31m-  },[m
[31m-[m
[31m-  ignorePermissionErrors: false,[m
[31m-  atomic: true // or a custom 'atomicity delay', in milliseconds (default 100)[m
[31m-});[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-`chokidar.watch(paths, [options])`[m
[31m-[m
[31m-* `paths` (string or array of strings). Paths to files, dirs to be watched[m
[31m-recursively, or glob patterns.[m
[31m-    - Note: globs must not contain windows separators (`\`),[m
[31m-    because that's how they work by the standard —[m
[31m-    you'll need to replace them with forward slashes (`/`).[m
[31m-    - Note 2: for additional glob documentation, check out low-level[m
[31m-    library: [picomatch](https://github.com/micromatch/picomatch).[m
[31m-* `options` (object) Options object as defined below:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-#### Persistence[m
[31m-[m
[31m-* `persistent` (default: `true`). Indicates whether the process[m
[31m-should continue to run as long as files are being watched. If set to[m
[31m-`false` when using `fsevents` to watch, no more events will be emitted[m
[31m-after `ready`, even if the process continues to run.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-#### Path filtering[m
[31m-[m
[31m-* `ignored` ([anymatch](https://github.com/es128/anymatch)-compatible definition)[m
[31m-Defines files/paths to be ignored. The whole relative or absolute path is[m
[31m-tested, not just filename. If a function with two arguments is provided, it[m
[31m-gets called twice per path - once with a single argument (the path), second[m
[31m-time with two arguments (the path and the[m
[31m-[`fs.Stats`](https://nodejs.org/api/fs.html#fs_class_fs_stats)[m
[31m-object of that path).[m
[31m-* `ignoreInitial` (default: `false`). If set to `false` then `add`/`addDir` events are also emitted for matching paths while[m
[31m-instantiating the watching as chokidar discovers these file paths (before the `ready` event).[m
[31m-* `followSymlinks` (default: `true`). When `false`, only the[m
[31m-symlinks themselves will be watched for changes instead of following[m
[31m-the link references and bubbling events through the link's path.[m
[31m-* `cwd` (no default). The base directory from which watch `paths` are to be[m
[31m-derived. Paths emitted with events will be relative to this.[m
[31m-* `disableGlobbing` (default: `false`). If set to `true` then the strings passed to `.watch()` and `.add()` are treated as[m
[31m-literal path names, even if they look like globs.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-#### Performance[m
[31m-[m
[31m-* `usePolling` (default: `false`).[m
[31m-Whether to use fs.watchFile (backed by polling), or fs.watch. If polling[m
[31m-leads to high CPU utilization, consider setting this to `false`. It is[m
[31m-typically necessary to **set this to `true` to successfully watch files over[m
[31m-a network**, and it may be necessary to successfully watch files in other[m
[31m-non-standard situations. Setting to `true` explicitly on MacOS overrides the[m
[31m-`useFsEvents` default. You may also set the CHOKIDAR_USEPOLLING env variable[m
[31m-to true (1) or false (0) in order to override this option.[m
[31m-* _Polling-specific settings_ (effective when `usePolling: true`)[m
[31m-  * `interval` (default: `100`). Interval of file system polling, in milliseconds. You may also[m
[31m-    set the CHOKIDAR_INTERVAL env variable to override this option.[m
[31m-  * `binaryInterval` (default: `300`). Interval of file system[m
[31m-  polling for binary files.[m
[31m-  ([see list of binary extensions](https://github.com/sindresorhus/binary-extensions/blob/master/binary-extensions.json))[m
[31m-* `useFsEvents` (default: `true` on MacOS). Whether to use the[m
[31m-`fsevents` watching interface if available. When set to `true` explicitly[m
[31m-and `fsevents` is available this supercedes the `usePolling` setting. When[m
[31m-set to `false` on MacOS, `usePolling: true` becomes the default.[m
[31m-* `alwaysStat` (default: `false`). If relying upon the[m
[31m-[`fs.Stats`](https://nodejs.org/api/fs.html#fs_class_fs_stats)[m
[31m-object that may get passed with `add`, `addDir`, and `change` events, set[m
[31m-this to `true` to ensure it is provided even in cases where it wasn't[m
[31m-already available from the underlying watch events.[m
[31m-* `depth` (default: `undefined`). If set, limits how many levels of[m
[31m-subdirectories will be traversed.[m
[31m-* `awaitWriteFinish` (default: `false`).[m
[31m-By default, the `add` event will fire when a file first appears on disk, before[m
[31m-the entire file has been written. Furthermore, in some cases some `change`[m
[31m-events will be emitted while the file is being written. In some cases,[m
[31m-especially when watching for large files there will be a need to wait for the[m
[31m-write operation to finish before responding to a file creation or modification.[m
[31m-Setting `awaitWriteFinish` to `true` (or a truthy value) will poll file size,[m
[31m-holding its `add` and `change` events until the size does not change for a[m
[31m-configurable amount of time. The appropriate duration setting is heavily[m
[31m-dependent on the OS and hardware. For accurate detection this parameter should[m
[31m-be relatively high, making file watching much less responsive.[m
[31m-Use with caution.[m
[31m-  * *`options.awaitWriteFinish` can be set to an object in order to adjust[m
[31m-  timing params:*[m
[31m-  * `awaitWriteFinish.stabilityThreshold` (default: 2000). Amount of time in[m
[31m-  milliseconds for a file size to remain constant before emitting its event.[m
[31m-  * `awaitWriteFinish.pollInterval` (default: 100). File size polling interval, in milliseconds.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-#### Errors[m
[31m-[m
[31m-* `ignorePermissionErrors` (default: `false`). Indicates whether to watch files[m
[31m-that don't have read permissions if possible. If watching fails due to `EPERM`[m
[31m-or `EACCES` with this set to `true`, the errors will be suppressed silently.[m
[31m-* `atomic` (default: `true` if `useFsEvents` and `usePolling` are `false`).[m
[31m-Automatically filters out artifacts that occur when using editors that use[m
[31m-"atomic writes" instead of writing directly to the source file. If a file is[m
[31m-re-added within 100 ms of being deleted, Chokidar emits a `change` event[m
[31m-rather than `unlink` then `add`. If the default of 100 ms does not work well[m
[31m-for you, you can override it by setting `atomic` to a custom value, in[m
[31m-milliseconds.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### Methods & Events[m
[31m-[m
[31m-`chokidar.watch()` produces an instance of `FSWatcher`. Methods of `FSWatcher`:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-* `.add(path / paths)`: Add files, directories, or glob patterns for tracking.[m
[31m-Takes an array of strings or just one string.[m
[31m-* `.on(event, callback)`: Listen for an FS event.[m
[31m-Available events: `add`, `addDir`, `change`, `unlink`, `unlinkDir`, `ready`,[m
[31m-`raw`, `error`.[m
[31m-Additionally `all` is available which gets emitted with the underlying event[m
[31m-name and path for every event other than `ready`, `raw`, and `error`.  `raw` is internal, use it carefully.[m
[31m-* `.unwatch(path / paths)`: Stop watching files, directories, or glob patterns.[m
[31m-Takes an array of strings or just one string.[m
[31m-* `.close()`: **async** Removes all listeners from watched files. Asynchronous, returns Promise. Use with `await` to ensure bugs don't happen.[m
[31m-* `.getWatched()`: Returns an object representing all the paths on the file[m
[31m-system being watched by this `FSWatcher` instance. The object's keys are all the[m
[31m-directories (using absolute paths unless the `cwd` option was used), and the[m
[31m-values are arrays of the names of the items contained in each directory.[m
