[31m-## Windows[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**Please only use forward-slashes in glob expressions.**[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Though windows uses either `/` or `\` as its path separator, only `/`[m
[31m-characters are used by this glob implementation. You must use[m
[31m-forward-slashes **only** in glob expressions. Back-slashes in patterns[m
[31m-will always be interpreted as escape characters, not path separators.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Note that `\` or `/` _will_ be interpreted as path separators in paths on[m
[31m-Windows, and will match against `/` in glob expressions.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-So just always use `/` in patterns.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### UNC Paths[m
[31m-[m
[31m-On Windows, UNC paths like `//?/c:/...` or[m
[31m-`//ComputerName/Share/...` are handled specially.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-- Patterns starting with a double-slash followed by some[m
[31m-  non-slash characters will preserve their double-slash. As a[m
[31m-  result, a pattern like `//*` will match `//x`, but not `/x`.[m
[31m-- Patterns staring with `//?/<drive letter>:` will _not_ treat[m
[31m-  the `?` as a wildcard character. Instead, it will be treated[m
[31m-  as a normal string.[m
[31m-- Patterns starting with `//?/<drive letter>:/...` will match[m
[31m-  file paths starting with `<drive letter>:/...`, and vice versa,[m
[31m-  as if the `//?/` was not present. This behavior only is[m
[31m-  present when the drive letters are a case-insensitive match to[m
[31m-  one another. The remaining portions of the path/pattern are[m
[31m-  compared case sensitively, unless `nocase:true` is set.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Note that specifying a UNC path using `\` characters as path[m
[31m-separators is always allowed in the file path argument, but only[m
[31m-allowed in the pattern argument when `windowsPathsNoEscape: true`[m
[31m-is set in the options.[m
