[31m-## Running non-node scripts[m
[31m-[m
[31m-nodemon can also be used to execute and monitor other programs. nodemon will read the file extension of the script being run and monitor that extension instead of `.js` if there's no `nodemon.json`:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```bash[m
[31m-nodemon --exec "python -v" ./app.py[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Now nodemon will run `app.py` with python in verbose mode (note that if you're not passing args to the exec program, you don't need the quotes), and look for new or modified files with the `.py` extension.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### Default executables[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Using the `nodemon.json` config file, you can define your own default executables using the `execMap` property. This is particularly useful if you're working with a language that isn't supported by default by nodemon.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-To add support for nodemon to know about the `.pl` extension (for Perl), the `nodemon.json` file would add:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```json[m
[31m-{[m
[31m-  "execMap": {[m
[31m-    "pl": "perl"[m
[31m-  }[m
[31m-}[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Now running the following, nodemon will know to use `perl` as the executable:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```bash[m
[31m-nodemon script.pl[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-It's generally recommended to use the global `nodemon.json` to add your own `execMap` options. However, if there's a common default that's missing, this can be merged in to the project so that nodemon supports it by default, by changing [default.js](https://github.com/remy/nodemon/blob/master/lib/config/defaults.js) and sending a pull request.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Monitoring multiple directories[m
[31m-[m
[31m-By default nodemon monitors the current working directory. If you want to take control of that option, use the `--watch` option to add specific paths:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```bash[m
[31m-nodemon --watch app --watch libs app/server.js[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Now nodemon will only restart if there are changes in the `./app` or `./libs` directory. By default nodemon will traverse sub-directories, so there's no need in explicitly including sub-directories.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Nodemon also supports unix globbing, e.g `--watch './lib/*'`. The globbing pattern must be quoted. For advanced globbing, [see `picomatch` documentation](https://github.com/micromatch/picomatch#advanced-globbing), the library that nodemon uses through `chokidar` (which in turn uses it through `anymatch`).[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Specifying extension watch list[m
[31m-[m
[31m-By default, nodemon looks for files with the `.js`, `.mjs`, `.coffee`, `.litcoffee`, and `.json` extensions. If you use the `--exec` option and monitor `app.py` nodemon will monitor files with the extension of `.py`. However, you can specify your own list with the `-e` (or `--ext`) switch like so:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```bash[m
[31m-nodemon -e js,pug[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Now nodemon will restart on any changes to files in the directory (or subdirectories) with the extensions `.js`, `.pug`.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Ignoring files[m
[31m-[m
[31m-By default, nodemon will only restart when a `.js` JavaScript file changes. In some cases you will want to ignore some specific files, directories or file patterns, to prevent nodemon from prematurely restarting your application.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-This can be done via the command line:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```bash[m
[31m-nodemon --ignore lib/ --ignore tests/[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Or specific files can be ignored:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```bash[m
[31m-nodemon --ignore lib/app.js[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Patterns can also be ignored (but be sure to quote the arguments):[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```bash[m
[31m-nodemon --ignore 'lib/*.js'[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**Important** the ignore rules are patterns matched to the full absolute path, and this determines how many files are monitored. If using a wild card glob pattern, it needs to be used as `**` or omitted entirely. For example, `nodemon --ignore '**/test/**'` will work, whereas `--ignore '*/test/*'` will not.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Note that by default, nodemon will ignore the `.git`, `node_modules`, `bower_components`, `.nyc_output`, `coverage` and `.sass-cache` directories and *add* your ignored patterns to the list. If you want to indeed watch a directory like `node_modules`, you need to [override the underlying default ignore rules](https://github.com/remy/nodemon/blob/master/faq.md#overriding-the-underlying-default-ignore-rules).[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Application isn't restarting[m
[31m-[m
[31m-In some networked environments (such as a container running nodemon reading across a mounted drive), you will need to use the `legacyWatch: true` which enables Chokidar's polling.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Via the CLI, use either `--legacy-watch` or `-L` for short:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```bash[m
[31m-nodemon -L[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Though this should be a last resort as it will poll every file it can find.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Delaying restarting[m
[31m-[m
[31m-In some situations, you may want to wait until a number of files have changed. The timeout before checking for new file changes is 1 second. If you're uploading a number of files and it's taking some number of seconds, this could cause your app to restart multiple times unnecessarily.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-To add an extra throttle, or delay restarting, use the `--delay` command:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```bash[m
[31m-nodemon --delay 10 server.js[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-For more precision, milliseconds can be specified.  Either as a float:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```bash[m
[31m-nodemon --delay 2.5 server.js[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Or using the time specifier (ms):[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```bash[m
[31m-nodemon --delay 2500ms server.js[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-The delay figure is number of seconds (or milliseconds, if specified) to delay before restarting. So nodemon will only restart your app the given number of seconds after the *last* file change.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-If you are setting this value in `nodemon.json`, the value will always be interpreted in milliseconds. E.g., the following are equivalent:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```bash[m
[31m-nodemon --delay 2.5[m
[31m-[m
[31m-{[m
[31m-  "delay": 2500[m
[31m-}[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Gracefully reloading down your script[m
[31m-[m
[31m-It is possible to have nodemon send any signal that you specify to your application.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```bash[m
[31m-nodemon --signal SIGHUP server.js[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Your application can handle the signal as follows.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```js[m
[31m-process.on("SIGHUP", function () {[m
[31m-  reloadSomeConfiguration();[m
[31m-  process.kill(process.pid, "SIGTERM");[m
[31m-})[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Please note that nodemon will send this signal to every process in the process tree.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-If you are using `cluster`, then each workers (as well as the master) will receive the signal. If you wish to terminate all workers on receiving a `SIGHUP`, a common pattern is to catch the `SIGHUP` in the master, and forward `SIGTERM` to all workers, while ensuring that all workers ignore `SIGHUP`.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```js[m
[31m-if (cluster.isMaster) {[m
[31m-  process.on("SIGHUP", function () {[m
[31m-    for (const worker of Object.values(cluster.workers)) {[m
[31m-      worker.process.kill("SIGTERM");[m
[31m-    }[m
[31m-  });[m
[31m-} else {[m
[31m-  process.on("SIGHUP", function() {})[m
[31m-}[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Controlling shutdown of your script[m
[31m-[m
[31m-nodemon sends a kill signal to your application when it sees a file update. If you need to clean up on shutdown inside your script you can capture the kill signal and handle it yourself.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-The following example will listen once for the `SIGUSR2` signal (used by nodemon to restart), run the clean up process and then kill itself for nodemon to continue control:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```js[m
[31m-// important to use `on` and not `once` as nodemon can re-send the kill signal[m
[31m-process.on('SIGUSR2', function () {[m
[31m-  gracefulShutdown(function () {[m
[31m-    process.kill(process.pid, 'SIGTERM');[m
[31m-  });[m
[31m-});[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Note that the `process.kill` is *only* called once your shutdown jobs are complete. Hat tip to [Benjie Gillam](http://www.benjiegillam.com/2011/08/node-js-clean-restart-and-faster-development-with-nodemon/) for writing this technique up.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Triggering events when nodemon state changes[m
[31m-[m
[31m-If you want growl like notifications when nodemon restarts or to trigger an action when an event happens, then you can either `require` nodemon or add event actions to your `nodemon.json` file.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-For example, to trigger a notification on a Mac when nodemon restarts, `nodemon.json` looks like this:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```json[m
[31m-{[m
[31m-  "events": {[m
[31m-    "restart": "osascript -e 'display notification \"app restarted\" with title \"nodemon\"'"[m
[31m-  }[m
[31m-}[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-A full list of available events is listed on the [event states wiki](https://github.com/remy/nodemon/wiki/Events#states). Note that you can bind to both states and messages.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Pipe output to somewhere else[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```js[m
[31m-nodemon({[m
[31m-  script: ...,[m
[31m-  stdout: false // important: this tells nodemon not to output to console[m
[31m-}).on('readable', function() { // the `readable` event indicates that data is ready to pick up[m
[31m-  this.stdout.pipe(fs.createWriteStream('output.txt'));[m
[31m-  this.stderr.pipe(fs.createWriteStream('err.txt'));[m
[31m-});[m
[31m-```[m
