[31m-# ms[m
[31m-[m
[31m-![CI](https://github.com/vercel/ms/workflows/CI/badge.svg)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Use this package to easily convert various time formats to milliseconds.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Examples[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```js[m
[31m-ms('2 days')  // 172800000[m
[31m-ms('1d')      // 86400000[m
[31m-ms('10h')     // 36000000[m
[31m-ms('2.5 hrs') // 9000000[m
[31m-ms('2h')      // 7200000[m
[31m-ms('1m')      // 60000[m
[31m-ms('5s')      // 5000[m
[31m-ms('1y')      // 31557600000[m
[31m-ms('100')     // 100[m
[31m-ms('-3 days') // -259200000[m
[31m-ms('-1h')     // -3600000[m
[31m-ms('-200')    // -200[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### Convert from Milliseconds[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```js[m
[31m-ms(60000)             // "1m"[m
[31m-ms(2 * 60000)         // "2m"[m
[31m-ms(-3 * 60000)        // "-3m"[m
[31m-ms(ms('10 hours'))    // "10h"[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### Time Format Written-Out[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```js[m
[31m-ms(60000, { long: true })             // "1 minute"[m
[31m-ms(2 * 60000, { long: true })         // "2 minutes"[m
[31m-ms(-3 * 60000, { long: true })        // "-3 minutes"[m
[31m-ms(ms('10 hours'), { long: true })    // "10 hours"[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Features[m
[31m-[m
[31m-- Works both in [Node.js](https://nodejs.org) and in the browser[m
[31m-- If a number is supplied to `ms`, a string with a unit is returned[m
[31m-- If a string that contains the number is supplied, it returns it as a number (e.g.: it returns `100` for `'100'`)[m
[31m-- If you pass a string with a number and a valid unit, the number of equivalent milliseconds is returned[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Related Packages[m
[31m-[m
[31m-- [ms.macro](https://github.com/knpwrs/ms.macro) - Run `ms` as a macro at build-time.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Caught a Bug?[m
[31m-[m
[31m-1. [Fork](https://help.github.com/articles/fork-a-repo/) this repository to your own GitHub account and then [clone](https://help.github.com/articles/cloning-a-repository/) it to your local device[m
[31m-2. Link the package to the global module directory: `npm link`[m
[31m-3. Within the module you want to test your local development instance of ms, just link it to the dependencies: `npm link ms`. Instead of the default one from npm, Node.js will now use your clone of ms![m
[31m-[m
[31m-As always, you can run the tests using: `npm test`[m
