[31m-## Globbing features[m
[31m-[m
[31m-* [Basic globbing](#basic-globbing) (Wildcard matching)[m
[31m-* [Advanced globbing](#advanced-globbing) (extglobs, posix brackets, brace matching)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### Basic globbing[m
[31m-[m
[31m-| **Character** | **Description** |[m
[31m-| --- | --- |[m
[31m-| `*` | Matches any character zero or more times, excluding path separators. Does _not match_ path separators or hidden files or directories ("dotfiles"), unless explicitly enabled by setting the `dot` option to `true`. |[m
[31m-| `**` | Matches any character zero or more times, including path separators. Note that `**` will only match path separators (`/`, and `\\` on Windows) when they are the only characters in a path segment. Thus, `foo**/bar` is equivalent to `foo*/bar`, and `foo/a**b/bar` is equivalent to `foo/a*b/bar`, and _more than two_ consecutive stars in a glob path segment are regarded as _a single star_. Thus, `foo/***/bar` is equivalent to `foo/*/bar`. |[m
[31m-| `?` | Matches any character excluding path separators one time. Does _not match_ path separators or leading dots.  |[m
[31m-| `[abc]` | Matches any characters inside the brackets. For example, `[abc]` would match the characters `a`, `b` or `c`, and nothing else. |[m
[31m-[m
[31m-#### Matching behavior vs. Bash[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Picomatch's matching features and expected results in unit tests are based on Bash's unit tests and the Bash 4.3 specification, with the following exceptions:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-* Bash will match `foo/bar/baz` with `*`. Picomatch only matches nested directories with `**`.[m
[31m-* Bash greedily matches with negated extglobs. For example, Bash 4.3 says that `!(foo)*` should match `foo` and `foobar`, since the trailing `*` bracktracks to match the preceding pattern. This is very memory-inefficient, and IMHO, also incorrect. Picomatch would return `false` for both `foo` and `foobar`.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-<br>[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### Advanced globbing[m
[31m-[m
[31m-* [extglobs](#extglobs)[m
[31m-* [POSIX brackets](#posix-brackets)[m
[31m-* [Braces](#brace-expansion)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-#### Extglobs[m
[31m-[m
[31m-| **Pattern** | **Description** |[m
[31m-| --- | --- |[m
[31m-| `@(pattern)` | Match _only one_ consecutive occurrence of `pattern` |[m
[31m-| `*(pattern)` | Match _zero or more_ consecutive occurrences of `pattern` |[m
[31m-| `+(pattern)` | Match _one or more_ consecutive occurrences of `pattern` |[m
[31m-| `?(pattern)` | Match _zero or **one**_ consecutive occurrences of `pattern` |[m
[31m-| `!(pattern)` | Match _anything but_ `pattern` |[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**Examples**[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```js[m
[31m-const pm = require('picomatch');[m
[31m-[m
[31m-// *(pattern) matches ZERO or more of "pattern"[m
[31m-console.log(pm.isMatch('a', 'a*(z)')); // true[m
[31m-console.log(pm.isMatch('az', 'a*(z)')); // true[m
[31m-console.log(pm.isMatch('azzz', 'a*(z)')); // true[m
[31m-[m
[31m-// +(pattern) matches ONE or more of "pattern"[m
[31m-console.log(pm.isMatch('a', 'a*(z)')); // true[m
[31m-console.log(pm.isMatch('az', 'a*(z)')); // true[m
[31m-console.log(pm.isMatch('azzz', 'a*(z)')); // true[m
[31m-[m
[31m-// supports multiple extglobs[m
[31m-console.log(pm.isMatch('foo.bar', '!(foo).!(bar)')); // false[m
[31m-[m
[31m-// supports nested extglobs[m
[31m-console.log(pm.isMatch('foo.bar', '!(!(foo)).!(!(bar))')); // true[m
[31m-[m
[31m-// risky quantified extglobs are treated literally by default[m
[31m-console.log(pm.makeRe('+(a|aa)'));[m
[31m-//=> /^(?:\+\(a\|aa\))$/[m
[31m-[m
[31m-// increase the limit to allow a small amount of nested quantified extglobs[m
[31m-console.log(pm.isMatch('aaa', '+(+(a))', { maxExtglobRecursion: 1 })); // true[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-#### POSIX brackets[m
[31m-[m
[31m-POSIX classes are disabled by default. Enable this feature by setting the `posix` option to true.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**Enable POSIX bracket support**[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```js[m
[31m-console.log(pm.makeRe('[[:word:]]+', { posix: true }));[m
[31m-//=> /^(?:(?=.)[A-Za-z0-9_]+\/?)$/[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**Supported POSIX classes**[m
[31m-[m
[31m-The following named POSIX bracket expressions are supported:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-* `[:alnum:]` - Alphanumeric characters, equ `[a-zA-Z0-9]`[m
[31m-* `[:alpha:]` - Alphabetical characters, equivalent to `[a-zA-Z]`.[m
[31m-* `[:ascii:]` - ASCII characters, equivalent to `[\\x00-\\x7F]`.[m
[31m-* `[:blank:]` - Space and tab characters, equivalent to `[ \\t]`.[m
[31m-* `[:cntrl:]` - Control characters, equivalent to `[\\x00-\\x1F\\x7F]`.[m
[31m-* `[:digit:]` - Numerical digits, equivalent to `[0-9]`.[m
[31m-* `[:graph:]` - Graph characters, equivalent to `[\\x21-\\x7E]`.[m
[31m-* `[:lower:]` - Lowercase letters, equivalent to `[a-z]`.[m
[31m-* `[:print:]` - Print characters, equivalent to `[\\x20-\\x7E ]`.[m
[31m-* `[:punct:]` - Punctuation and symbols, equivalent to `[\\-!"#$%&\'()\\*+,./:;<=>?@[\\]^_`{|}~]`.[m
[31m-* `[:space:]` - Extended space characters, equivalent to `[ \\t\\r\\n\\v\\f]`.[m
[31m-* `[:upper:]` - Uppercase letters, equivalent to `[A-Z]`.[m
[31m-* `[:word:]` -  Word characters (letters, numbers and underscores), equivalent to `[A-Za-z0-9_]`.[m
[31m-* `[:xdigit:]` - Hexadecimal digits, equivalent to `[A-Fa-f0-9]`.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-See the [Bash Reference Manual](https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Pattern-Matching.html) for more information.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### Braces[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Picomatch does not do brace expansion. For [brace expansion](https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Brace-Expansion.html) and advanced matching with braces, use [micromatch](https://github.com/micromatch/micromatch) instead. Picomatch has very basic support for braces.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### Matching special characters as literals[m
[31m-[m
[31m-If you wish to match the following special characters in a filepath, and you want to use these characters in your glob pattern, they must be escaped with backslashes or quotes:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**Special Characters**[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Some characters that are used for matching in regular expressions are also regarded as valid file path characters on some platforms.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-To match any of the following characters as literals: `$^*+?()[][m
[31m-[m
[31m-Examples:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```js[m
[31m-console.log(pm.makeRe('foo/bar \\(1\\)'));[m
[31m-console.log(pm.makeRe('foo/bar \\(1\\)'));[m
[31m-```[m
